{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head 940166311e8c3db60c0243eb240fe4561ed60952, as source. **The J2 acceptance has not happened, and this approval does not meet it.** No deployed Worker, real room, Artifacts repository or desktop AI app ran this code, as your note states. All five points hold. Two small items follow them.\n\n**1. The rebase design: sound, and every statement can only under-claim.** I read `InterdiffWalk` against each case that could give a false attribution.\n- **Edited.** Bases agree at a path, including both absent (`sameEntry(null, null)`), so only the change explains a difference. The statement is true if the base is true.\n- **Changed only by the new base.** Each integration holds its own base's entry, by object ID and mode. True.\n- **The same edit.** Keys match on modes, names and the whole hunk with its context, and they leave out only the line numbers. True. A base edit next to the change gives a different key, so it reads as \"cannot separate\", the safe side.\n- **Cannot separate.** This is everything else. A binary file, a directory on one side and a submodule all have `key: null`, so `sameKey` is false and they can never reach \"the same edit\" (`diff.ts:189-197`). This is the case that I most wanted to break, and it holds.\n- **Paths where the two integrations agree** are not walked. They show no difference between versions, and nothing is claimed about them.\n- **\"Makes no edit of its own\"** prints only when nothing was truncated and both of the shown groups are empty (`:469`).\n\nYour reading of \"the reviewer's change\" as the change under review fits the scene. Rejecting the merge simulation is right: a wrong merge would state a false edit as fact.\n\n**2. The trust in `base`: acceptable, and I confirmed the guard by reading.** `reservation.ts:224` refuses a manifest whose base is not the destination's head, and `:225` requires that the base is the integration's first parent. So a false base cannot land, though it can mislead a reviewer until then: \"Changed only by the new base\" can hide an edit, and a reviewer can spend a review on such a version. View diff already has this trust. I would not hold J2 for it, but the card states these groups as fact. If a slice later shows the destination's head on the card, a reviewer could see that a base is not the head before reading. That is the planner's call. Nobody ran the refusal in this head.\n\n**3. e143d731: correct.** The stop's words at `:279-280` now name the second patch. An omitted line goes to \"Not read\" through `omissions`, once `setBases` has run, and it stands under no heading that names who changed it. The 4 controls distinguish. The note says plainly that the red run and the 26 earlier controls stayed at 4b885c8b. Since e143d731 changes the words of a stop, moves those lines and guards one refusal, its own controls and a new run of the path are enough.\n\n**4. The S6 change: right, with one limit.** Held answers are keyed by `[card.identity, read]`, so a moved version misses the key and signs anew, and each answer's commits cannot change. The golden comment now says what changed.\n\nThe limit: a **truncated** answer is held too. A stop on the time bound (`milliseconds`) depends on the moment, and a press would show the held partial answer and sign nothing, so the person cannot retry it until the view reopens or four later reads push it out. The words stay true, because the band says \"Truncated\", so this does not hold the head. A later edit could hold only an answer that no time bound cut.\n\n**5. `views.mjs`: right.** A replacement string reads `$\u0026`, and function replacers do not. Both replacements use them, and `view-resources.test.ts` passes.\n\n**Security.** The browser sends only an opaque action ID, and `#exactRead` maps it to server-held commits and paths, with `ordinal` giving -1 on anything else. `hosted.ts` supplies no view source and no reader, which matches \"open nothing\". `commitIdFault` checks every commit before a signature. Each read spends one credential, which goes only to `remote` and enters no answer. Repository text and paths stay data: `shownPath` for names and `pre` for text, as your note says. I did not trace how a desktop app passes a `view_act` result to its model, which no run has shown and which your witness script should watch. A key that loses its membership keeps a held answer on the screen until the view closes. That answer is text that it already read.\n\n**A small one.** `#readExact` ignores `#showRead`'s result on a held answer and says \"shown again\". A held answer already fit the card, so I expect no failure there.\n\n**For the planner, as you say.** A landing closes request 3cb0b014, so the J2 acceptance needs its own home. The witness script needs a room with the J4 change definition active, and J4's remaining phases, and it still has three unshown facts: whether the app renders this card, whether Artifacts gives a read token an unadvertised staged commit, and whether the app takes about 90 KiB. If Artifacts refuses, the page says so and this head needs a change. The reader's whole path so far is the composed proxy.\n\n**Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` is ef9b6bf0…, every digest verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's `head.txt` is e143d731, exit 0, with 3532 and 1013 tests. All 26 controls and all 4 later controls read `distinguishes`. The head after the gate adds only the note and its manifest. I ran `interdiff.test.ts` and `diff.test.ts` (21 passed), `view-sessions.test.ts` and `view-resources.test.ts` (14 passed), `change-view.scope.test.ts` (17 passed), `apps/views/test/s4.test.mjs` (17 pass), `security-entry-points` (10 pass), check-notes (0 warnings), check-doc-links (1001, 0 broken) and check-commit-messages (4, 0 flagged). The worktree is clean. I did not rerun the controls or the probe, and I did not read `packages/mcp/browser/main.ts` line by line. `views-browser.mjs` fails at main and at this head at the same step, as the note states, so no run witnesses the fixture past it.\n\n**Architecture.** One `ExactRead` type replaces the single diff read, `Walk` keeps both walks on one set of bounds, and no new tool appears. No hosted route changes.\n\n**Simplification.** `leftOut` has several branches, because each states what a stop can leave out. I would keep them, since this review's own question is whether the words are true.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ceea66292df5ff7867d3e47100b5c4aaecd79964","binding":"assigned","head":"940166311e8c3db60c0243eb240fe4561ed60952","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1ba727fb1b9b376e07fd39925268432ac32a2b5d\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6e0f42611d9276df831098a6bd3ed065ed911856\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1ba727fb1b9b376e07fd39925268432ac32a2b5d","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}